
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that keeps dev setup to [38;5;153muv[39m and nothing else running.

  Last question before I put a design together. This one belongs to sub-project 3, but it lands in the task schema now,
  so it's cheaper to answer here than to retrofit.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what makes a user "care about" a task. Implicit derivation (compute it from roles) needs no extra storage
  but users can't control it; explicit subscription needs a table and UI but is the only way someone opts out of a noisy
  task. This shapes the schema in sub-project 1 either way.

  - [1mAssignee + creator, implicit[0m — simplest. No subscription storage, no UI. Two columns on the task and the
    notification routing falls out for free. Can't watch a task you're not on, can't mute one you are.
  - [1mImplicit roles + explicit watch/mute overrides[0m — the common product shape (GitHub, Jira, Linear). Auto-subscribe on
    assign/create/comment, with an explicit override. Needs a subscription table, but it's a small one, and adding it
    later means backfilling.
  - [1mFully explicit watchers only[0m — nothing is automatic; you subscribe or you hear nothing. Cleanest semantics, quietest
    system, but users routinely miss things assigned to them.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the second. The subscription table is cheap to add now and awkward to add later, and
  "auto-subscribe with an escape hatch" is the shape users already expect. For sub-project 1 it means the task model
  carries [38;5;153mcreator[39m and [38;5;153massignee[39m, and I'd define the subscription table in the notifications spec rather than building it
  yet.
